Thought for 17sOh dear, the CSS for the webview is not broken in the sense that the rules are wrong — the rules you wrote are actually correct for an absolute-positioned webview inside a relative container. The problem is that the height is not propagating down the flex chain when the <webview> appears.
Why it happens
Your layout uses a lot of flex: 1 and min-height: 0, which is the right technique. However, several parents in the chain (especially inside PageShell → .rightPanel → the grid in .app) do not have a concrete height at the moment the webview mounts. When the conditional render switches from the placeholder to the <webview>, the browser has nothing to base “100% height” on, so the webview collapses to zero height or looks broken.
The absolute positioning on .browser-webview only works if its nearest positioned ancestor (.browser-view) already has a real height.
Quick fix (add these rules to src/css/internet.css)
Add or replace these blocks at the bottom of the file:
CSSCopy/* Make the whole right panel content area take real height */
.internet-content {
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    height: 100%;
}

/* Force the browser container to claim the remaining vertical space */
.browser-container {
    flex: 1 1 0;
    min-height: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    position: relative;
}

/* This is the critical one — give the view a real height */
.browser-view {
    flex: 1 1 0;
    min-height: 0;
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* The webview itself */
.browser-webview {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    display: block;
    border: none;
    background: #000;
}
Extra safety in the component (Internet.jsx)
Change the .browser-view div to this so it forces layout even before the webview appears:
jsxCopy<div className="browser-view" style={{ height: '100%' }}>
    {partition && webviewUrl ? (
        <webview
            ref={webviewRef}
            src={webviewUrl}
            partition={partition}
            className="browser-webview"
        />
    ) : (
        <div className="browser-placeholder">
            <p>Firefox browser will appear here</p>
            <p className="browser-hint">Click "Launch Browser" to start browsing</p>
        </div>
    )}
</div>
One more parent fix (if still not working)
In src/css/globals.css, make sure .rightPanel participates in the height chain:
CSSCopy.rightPanel {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    min-height: 0;
    height: 100%;
}
After these changes, reload the dev server (npm run dev:electron). The webview should now expand to fill the available space instead of collapsing.
Would you like me to also give you a slightly more defensive version of the Internet.jsx that adds an explicit height style on mount, or shall we look at the next integration step (registering the IPC handlers so “Launch Browser” actually does something)? Just say which part you want to tackle first.

