Oh dear, thank you for clarifying. The big dump shows that you have written most of the Firefox browser + XScraper feature (the Internet.jsx page, the extension, the IPC handlers, and the documentation), but it is not yet wired into the running Electron app. That is why it feels “not integrated yet”.
Here is a calm, practical checklist of exactly what still needs to be connected so the browser actually opens inside Emerald Utilities.
1. Enable webview in the main process (most important)
Open src/heavensgate.js (your Electron main file).
Find where you create the BrowserWindow and make sure webviewTag: true is set:
JavaScriptCopyconst mainWindow = new BrowserWindow({
  webPreferences: {
    preload: path.join(__dirname, 'preload.js'),
    webviewTag: true,           // ← this line must be present
    nodeIntegration: false,
    contextIsolation: true,
    // ... your other settings
  }
});
Without this line the <webview> tag in Internet.jsx will be ignored.
2. Register the XScraper IPC handlers
The file src/utils/xscraperIpcHandlers.js already contains all the handlers (xscraper:launch-firefox, xscraper:check-firefox, etc.).
You must call the registration function from your main process.
In src/heavensgate.js (or wherever you register other IPC handlers), add:
JavaScriptCopyimport { registerXScraperIpcHandlers } from './utils/xscraperIpcHandlers.js';

// after you have the app, ipcMain, etc.
registerXScraperIpcHandlers({
  ipcMain,
  app,
  getDialogParentWindow: () => mainWindow,
  pushScriptLog: (msg) => { /* your existing log function */ }
});
3. Make sure the React router knows about the internet page
Check src/main.jsx. It should contain something like:
jsxCopyimport Internet from './pages/Internet.jsx';

function App() {
  const [route, setRoute] = useState('dashboard');

  return (
    <>
      {route === 'dashboard' && <Dashboard route={route} setRoute={setRoute} />}
      {route === 'internet' && <Internet route={route} setRoute={setRoute} />}
      {/* other routes */}
    </>
  );
}
If this routing is missing, the “Internet / Browser” button in the dashboard will do nothing.
4. Correct extension path when loading
In src/utils/xscraperIpcHandlers.js the code does:
JavaScriptCopyconst absoluteExtensionPath = path.join(app.getAppPath(), extensionPath);
await browserSession.loadExtension(absoluteExtensionPath);
When you call launchFirefox from Internet.jsx, you must pass the correct relative path to the extension folder.
A safe default is usually:
JavaScriptCopylaunchFirefox({ 
  url: 'https://grok.com',
  extensionPath: 'x_scraper'   // or wherever you placed the folder
})
Make sure the x_scraper folder (with manifest.json inside) ends up next to your built files or in app.getAppPath() after packaging.
5. Quick test order (do these one by one)

Add webviewTag: true and restart the dev server.
Register the xscraper IPC handlers.
Make sure setRoute('internet') actually renders <Internet />.
Click “Launch Browser” and watch the console in both the main process and the renderer for errors.
